      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose is unsafe as written.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day. That's well above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day, and many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. A single 2000 mg dose is also twice the usual maximum of 1,000 mg. This level of dosing risks serious liver injury, even in a patient with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some typical alternatives for a 70 kg adult with normal liver and kidney function:\n- **1000 mg PO every 8 hours** (3,000 mg/day). This is a common conservative choice.\n- **1000 mg PO every 6 hours** (4,000 mg/day). This is the standard maximum.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,600 mg/day).\n\nDid you mean one of these, or was 2000 mg/day or another total intended? Tell me the dose and frequency you want, and I'll enter it. Please also confirm it with your supervising resident or attending. I can add a note about the intended indication or a max daily dose if you'd like."
      }
     ],
